    detailedContent: "Building directly on the foundations laid in Computational Skills Session I, this follow-up session shifts from theoretical understanding to hands-on practice. Dr. Binayak Dutta and his team of lab assistants create a workshop-style environment where every student writes and runs code during the session.\n\nThe session begins with a brief recap of algorithmic concepts covered earlier, then quickly moves into new territory: Introduction to Data Structures. Using visual diagrams and step-by-step code walkthroughs, students are introduced to arrays, linked lists, stacks, and queues — the building blocks of every software system. The emphasis is on understanding when to use which structure, not just how to implement them.\n\nThe Linux Environment segment introduces students to the command line interface — an essential skill for any serious computing professional. Students learn basic commands (ls, cd, mkdir, cat, grep, chmod), file system navigation, and why Linux proficiency is valued by tech companies. The university's lab machines run Ubuntu, making this knowledge immediately practical.\n\nCollaborative coding is the session's culmination. Students are grouped into teams of 4-5 and given a mini-project challenge: build a simple command-line application (like a to-do list or basic calculator) within 30 minutes. This exercise mirrors real-world agile development sprints, introducing concepts like task division, version control (basic Git), and code integration.\n\nThe session concludes with a showcase where each team demonstrates their solution, fostering healthy competition and peer recognition. The best solutions are highlighted, and constructive feedback is provided by the faculty team.",
