    detailedContent: "Ms. Anisha Thampy's session addresses what is perhaps the most important yet least discussed aspect of postgraduate life: mental health and emotional wellbeing. The transition from undergraduate to postgraduate study — combined with relocation to a new city, academic pressure, and career anxiety — creates a perfect storm of stressors that can overwhelm even the most resilient students.\n\nThe session opens with a normalizing conversation: 'It's okay to not be okay.' Ms. Anisha shares statistics (anonymized) about the prevalence of anxiety, depression, and academic burnout among postgraduate students nationally, breaking the stigma around seeking help. She emphasizes that visiting a counselor is a sign of self-awareness, not weakness.\n\nPractical stress management techniques are taught through guided exercises:\n- Mindfulness meditation: A 5-minute guided breathing exercise that students can practice daily.\n- Time management frameworks: The Eisenhower Matrix and Pomodoro Technique adapted for PG academic schedules.\n- Sleep hygiene: The science of sleep and its impact on cognitive performance, memory consolidation, and emotional regulation.\n- Physical activity: How even 20 minutes of daily movement significantly reduces stress hormones and improves academic performance.\n\nThe university's counseling services are detailed: free, confidential sessions with trained counselors, available both in-person and online. Walk-in hours, appointment booking procedures, and the crisis helpline number are shared. Peer support networks — student volunteers trained in basic psychological first aid — are introduced.\n\nGender sensitivity receives dedicated attention. Ms. Anisha covers:\n- Creating inclusive classrooms where all genders feel respected and heard.\n- Understanding and preventing workplace (classroom) harassment.\n- The Internal Complaints Committee (ICC) and how to file complaints.\n- LGBTQ+ inclusivity: using correct pronouns, avoiding assumptions, and creating safe spaces.\n- The specific challenges faced by women in tech and strategies for creating equitable team dynamics.\n\nThe session concludes with each student writing a personal wellbeing commitment — a brief note to themselves about one self-care practice they will prioritize during their PG journey.",
